WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.740
I think most people think that

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:04.200
DJs are sort of mystical or magical.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.552
That they have access to special people, that

00:00:07.552 --> 00:00:11.088
they're gifted with skills behind the decks and

00:00:11.088 --> 00:00:14.200
have access to this music that nobody else has access to.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.200
I don't think that's true.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.165
I think that great DJs actually

00:00:17.165 --> 00:00:19.200
just have a message in their heart.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.960
And when they hear certain music that resonates with

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.387
that message, they just feel compelled to share and

00:00:24.387 --> 00:00:31.000
they have to get that message out to their audience.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Hi, I'm Avand.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.600
By day, I'm a software engineer.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.000
By night, I'm a DJ.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.200
I've been doing DJing for over 13 years.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.150
I've helped produce events, house

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.600
parties, festivals, renegades, you name it.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.400
And music's been a huge part of my life.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.071
So I first started DJing when my

00:00:45.071 --> 00:00:47.400
friend handed me down his two-channel mixer.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.180
I took to the tech really naturally,

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.600
especially having a background in software.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.567
But what really got me excited to DJ more and

00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:56.200
to take it more seriously were the

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.400
transformational experiences I had on the dance floor.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.133
Those types of moments where you realize we're all

00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:05.000
connected and we're all part of something bigger than ourselves.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.619
I think that's what got me really

00:01:06.619 --> 00:01:11.800
excited to take it more seriously.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.422
When people teach themselves how to DJ, they usually

00:01:14.422 --> 00:01:18.000
end up with gaps in their knowledge or develop bad habits.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.523
And it's really important, especially early on, to make

00:01:21.523 --> 00:01:24.600
sure that everything that they're learning is correct.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.700
That way, with each repetition,

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.400
they're reinforcing the correct behavior.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.077
It's very easy to get started as a DJ these days, but the

00:01:33.077 --> 00:01:36.600
tricky thing about your blind spots is that you can't see them.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.550
And I promise you that across the spectrum of the

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:44.100
stack, there are two to three X improvements to be

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.090
made in sound and signal flow and library and

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:50.600
workflow that most people just don't even think it's possible.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.000
Headroom is the zone between normal levels and max levels.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.600
Basically, it's about creative space so that

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.290
where you're operating normally has room in case

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:06.800
you need to push things or something wants to spike.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:13.400
So it's all about giving yourself some breathing room.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.800
When you stop thinking, you're able to be embodied.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.600
And that's where the dance floor lives.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.000
Your dancers are dancing, they're in their bodies.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.600
They're not thinking too much.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.340
The moment your brain turns on, you go out

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.649
of your body and you're no longer able to be

00:02:27.649 --> 00:02:30.400
in the room, feel the vibe and set the tone.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.192
When you deeply understand your

00:02:32.192 --> 00:02:34.800
craft, you will spend less time thinking.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.290
And when you're not thinking, you're able

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:43.200
to drop into your flow and be more creative.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.800
Headroom is not for beginners.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.233
It's for DJs that have already been doing it for a

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:49.300
while, but maybe recognize that there are some gaps

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.600
in their knowledge or some things that they could learn.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.228
Headroom is for DJs that want to get the most out of

00:02:54.228 --> 00:02:56.875
their equipment, the most out of their time and also want

00:02:56.875 --> 00:02:59.600
to bring the best possible experience to their dance floors.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.750
My hope for Headroom students is that they gain

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:05.580
confidence, that the curriculum really fills gaps in

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.980
their knowledge so that when they step into new

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:13.130
situations, they're able to perform competently and

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:16.400
confidently and bring the best experiences to the dance floor.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.340
The first cohort of Headroom launches in

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:20.620
October and because I want to keep the chorus

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:23.800
small and focused, I'm only opening up 12 spots.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.200
And I hope to see you there.

